On the podcast, I talk with Eric about how measurement dysfunction paralyzes growth, why diversifying channels for the sake of diversification actually hurts performance, and the futility of trying to interpret why ads win.

11
00:01:17,440 --> 00:01:19,760
Hey Eric, thanks so much for coming back on the podcast.

12
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:21,120
Great to be here, David.

13
00:01:21,360 --> 00:01:22,320
Thanks for inviting me back.

14
00:01:22,640 --> 00:01:35,120
All right, so we put a Google form out there for folks to ask questions and we got some really good questions in, and so I'm going to get to those in a sec, but I was kind of surprised that nobody asked about how to use it AI, what's going on with AI.

15
00:01:35,280 --> 00:01:38,000
I mean, it's just like that's all everybody's talking about on Twitter.

16
00:01:38,560 --> 00:01:41,680
Uh, so I was kind of expecting more questions around AI.

17
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:54,560
So I'm going to selfishly lead with my own question because I I do feel like we're we're at a bit of an inflection point where, you know, things are still early, but it feels like you're going to be left further and further behind if you're not at least starting to experiment.

18
00:01:54,960 --> 00:01:59,680
So what I wanted to ask you is like, where do you think the low hanging fruit is right now?

19
00:01:59,840 --> 00:02:11,040
Like teams that you're seeing be successful, what do you see them doing and using that's like effective today, not like, oh, this, you know, six months from now, this will actually get good, but like, what's good today?

20
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:14,640
There are a lot of dead ends that I see companies pursuing with AI, right?

21
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:26,320
And I think my advice here is, if you're going to embrace AI, and like, I think it's important to just kind of maybe take a step back and, and, and, and, kind of define what, what we're talking about when we say AI, right?

22
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:29,360
It's kind of like a catch all term at this point, but it shouldn't be though.

23
00:02:29,440 --> 00:02:30,000
It shouldn't be.

24
00:02:30,080 --> 00:02:38,320
So, like, and so I think when you're talking about AI, you're fundamentally talking about replacing A human decision making with some other mechanism.

25
00:02:39,120 --> 00:02:41,120
That's ultimately a big concept, what you're talking about.

26
00:02:42,880 --> 00:02:47,680
When people talk about AI, they tend to focus on the output.

27
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:51,280
I write a prompt in a chatbot and there's a bunch of text that gets generated.

28
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:56,320
Or I write a prompt in a text to image tool and there's some images that get created.

29
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:04,640
I think that's probably not the substance of how you transform your business with quote AI.

30
00:03:04,960 --> 00:03:05,520
Here's my advice.

31
00:03:06,160 --> 00:03:11,840
If you want to actually embrace this in a transformative way for your company, not like in a superficial way, first of all, start with first principles.

32
00:03:11,920 --> 00:03:12,880
What does AI mean for your business?

33
00:03:13,440 --> 00:03:18,160
So you know that I'm among other things that I do, I'm the Chief Strategy Officer at the Fabulous.

34
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:20,880
We make health and wellness apps, they're all subscription, right?

35
00:03:21,120 --> 00:03:23,760
So I have a genuine reason to be here on the subclub today.

36
00:03:24,080 --> 00:03:26,000
And so I'm leading this effort at Fabulous.

37
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:33,840
And what I did was like, I just worked with the founders and I was like, okay, let's start with what are our principles around AI, the use of AI within the company.

38
00:03:33,920 --> 00:03:36,560
Like, let's just define what we want to actually achieve with AI, right?

39
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:45,760
So this company Zapier, they released this document that they were using internally, which broke each organizational function down into a line item on this matrix.

40
00:03:46,080 --> 00:03:56,400
And then going across the columns were like the levels of implementation of AI from unacceptable or like no implementation to like the full embrace of AI.

41
00:03:56,720 --> 00:04:00,960
And they just kind of described what each team should do to kind of cascade across these columns.

42
00:04:01,120 --> 00:04:01,920
And so we did the same thing.

43
00:04:02,720 --> 00:04:04,400
This isn't like a top down mandate.

44
00:04:05,120 --> 00:04:09,840
We presented this to the teams and we said., right, fill it in.

45
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:35,200
You tell me what is the kind of complete sort of transformation of your organizational function through AI, and you tell me what is an unacceptable sort of non implementation of AI, and then we'll just kind of plan out a road map to get from no implementation to the complete transformative implementation, and then we'll decide what resources are needed.

46
00:04:35,840 --> 00:04:42,720
And so the teams themselves get to decide how they find that road map and pursue that road map and pursue that roadmap, but there is no option to not do that.

47
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:44,080
That's how I would start.

48
00:04:44,480 --> 00:05:01,280
Essentially, how do we absorb this into the culture of the company and make sure that every single functional team feels enabled to do this, and they also feel sort of like that they have the agency to define what that implementation looks like, because I think that's really important, right?

49
00:05:01,360 --> 00:05:10,400
So that's where I would start on the just kind of on the marketing side, I think, again, not focusing on outputs, but actually focusing on automation and marketing.

50
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:18,320
And replacing the kind of human effort with the kind of machine handled mechanism, thinking about anything that would move the needle, right?

51
00:05:18,560 --> 00:05:22,960
So if you want campaigns to be optimized in real time, that's not something you're going to build.

52
00:05:23,120 --> 00:05:29,200
You're going to rely on Facebook doing that, or Google doing that, or Pinterest doing that, or Amazon doing that, like they have those tools, or TikTok doing that, right?

53
00:05:29,360 --> 00:05:32,240
Like those are, those are sort of like platform imperatives.

54
00:05:32,480 --> 00:05:33,440
That's not for you to build.

55
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:39,360
And to your earlier point, those are actually machine learning models, not generative AI models.

56
00:05:39,680 --> 00:05:52,880
And I think Zuck tried to do that distinction on the latest earnings call is that the generative AI stuff is not what's powering the increase in the efficiency of ad spend on Meta.

57
00:05:53,440 --> 00:06:20,000
It's actually just getting better and better at the machine learning models, and that is a little bit of a catch 22 right now and kind of a confusion in the industry is that generative AI, chatbots, image generation, all these kinds of things, it seems so magical just like plaster it over everything, but there's a lot of things it's not good at like data analysis and other things where machine learning models, which now kind of just get lumped into this umbrella term of AI, like you were saying earlier.

58
00:06:20,400 --> 00:06:23,280
And so part of it is also like picking the right tool for the right thing.

59
00:06:23,520 --> 00:06:31,760
And so like, you know, to your point, the platforms are going to be so much better at that optimization and they're not using generative AI to do that.

60
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:38,560
They're using these machine learning models that they've built up over a decade to do that and all the data that you don't have and all that kind of stuff.

61
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:38,960
Yeah.

62
00:06:39,360 --> 00:06:40,560
And that's that's really important, right?

63
00:06:40,720 --> 00:06:40,960
The data.

64
00:06:41,120 --> 00:06:43,600
And so that's a great distinction I think a lot of people don't make it.

65
00:06:43,680 --> 00:06:43,840
Right.

66
00:06:43,920 --> 00:06:48,000
So what Meta calls those, those buckets, right, is core AI and Gen AI and Gen AI.

67
00:06:48,320 --> 00:06:57,120
I mean, to be fair, I mean, they have, they said they have two million advertisers using their Gen AI products for creative production, but a lot of that is still pretty superficial.

68
00:06:57,200 --> 00:07:03,920
I mean, they have animations, which is kind of a big deal, but a lot of it is still just like eComm, you know, sort of swapping out the backgrounds.

69
00:07:04,320 --> 00:07:11,760
But that, I mean, his, his point there was that hasn't yielded that much extra efficiency yet because they're still in the early stages of rolling it out.

70
00:07:12,160 --> 00:07:12,320
Right.

71
00:07:12,560 --> 00:07:14,000
But what has and what I call out.

72
00:07:14,320 --> 00:07:22,720
in my earnings analysis, but I also wrote another piece called Like AI is not the metaverse, saying like AI is really generating truly substantive efficiency for advertisers right now.

73
00:07:23,200 --> 00:07:27,360
It's not this far flung, far fetched sort of like destination.

74
00:07:27,440 --> 00:07:33,360
It's it's working on behalf of advertisers right now and generating sort of regular, you know, sort of improvements or optimizations to their ad campaigns.

75
00:07:33,760 --> 00:07:38,000
But those are those are things like Gem, which they've talked about, Lattice, Andromeda.

76
00:07:38,240 --> 00:07:41,760
And I had met a VP of AI on the show to just talk about all those tools.

77
00:07:42,160 --> 00:07:42,800
But it's really interesting.

78
00:07:42,960 --> 00:07:44,320
And it's like, yes, every quarter.

79
00:07:44,960 --> 00:07:47,840
They're pointing to those tools and saying they drove five percent more efficiency.

80
00:07:48,080 --> 00:07:52,000
I think that's something that people misinterpret because they say five percent, who cares?

81
00:07:52,880 --> 00:07:53,680
But no, but that compounds.

82
00:07:53,840 --> 00:07:56,800
That's five percent a quarter or that's five percent every six months.

83
00:07:56,880 --> 00:08:00,400
And then also when you unlock five percent efficiency in ad spend, what do you get?

84
00:08:00,560 --> 00:08:02,320
More ad spend the next cycle, right?

85
00:08:02,480 --> 00:08:03,520
And then so that grows faster.

86
00:08:03,680 --> 00:08:05,760
So I think people like sort of they don't know how to interpret that.

87
00:08:06,080 --> 00:08:08,160
So I would leave that kind of heavy lifting on the platforms.

88
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:08,960
You can't do that.

89
00:08:09,280 --> 00:08:10,320
So what can you do, right?

90
00:08:10,560 --> 00:08:14,320
And again, if you're not focusing on the output, you're not focusing on just creating a bunch of creative, right?

91
00:08:14,480 --> 00:08:16,000
That's probably not going to move the needle that much.

92
00:08:16,320 --> 00:08:17,280
What should you be focusing on?

93
00:08:17,520 --> 00:08:18,800
Just automating tasks, right?

94
00:08:19,120 --> 00:08:25,360
And a big one of those is the way I see people using AI is for creative prospecting.

95
00:08:25,760 --> 00:08:39,360
So just looking at what your competitors are doing, pulling that information into an S three bucket, whatever that information is, maybe it's ads, maybe it's other stuff, other things that are visible to you, and then using some sort of agent to sort of interpret it.

96
00:08:39,600 --> 00:08:42,160
And that, that would have been a full-time job three years ago.

97
00:08:42,240 --> 00:08:43,360
That, that's a full-time job.

98
00:08:43,520 --> 00:08:46,080
And, and every, you know, every big scaled app advertiser was doing that.

99
00:08:46,160 --> 00:08:47,920
They were looking at the Facebook Ads library.

100
00:08:48,480 --> 00:08:51,680
They were putting that into a DOC, into a Google Doc, and they were sending it around.

101
00:08:51,760 --> 00:08:54,000
It's like, hey, here's what our competitors are doing this week.

102
00:08:54,480 --> 00:08:55,600
What lessons can we take from that?

103
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:06,800
But now you can do that in an automated way using tools like LLMs to sort of interpret what you're seeing, interpret the kind of concepts from these ads and tell me why you think they're working.

104
00:09:07,120 --> 00:09:07,280
Right?

105
00:09:07,440 --> 00:09:09,680
And so that's a big thing that I think a lot of people don't appreciate.

106
00:09:09,840 --> 00:09:16,720
It's like creative synthesis, but like scaled way beyond what people were doing with like one person working on that maybe full time or half time.

107
00:09:17,280 --> 00:09:18,080
Interesting vectors.

108
00:09:18,080 --> 00:09:19,680
I think that's an interesting vector to be thinking about this on.

109
00:09:20,080 --> 00:09:28,400
And to your exact point is that one of the challenges we still have, especially with generative AI crunching numbers, is hallucination.

110
00:09:28,640 --> 00:09:45,040
Like I tried to just get ChatGPT, I used Pro, I used Thinking, I still haven't done deep research, but I just tried to get it to translate a list on a web page into like a formatted list that I wanted and exclude some things with a specific criteria.

111
00:09:45,920 --> 00:09:52,640
It did great for the first forty things on the list and then it just started to hallucinating and just making shit up, putting stuff in the wrong place.

112
00:09:53,600 --> 00:09:54,960
It just like really fell down.

113
00:09:55,600 --> 00:10:00,560
And so that's a really great example of like understanding the limitations of generative AI.

114
00:10:00,800 --> 00:10:08,080
You probably don't want to try and have an AI in between your ad buying and making decisions on pricing and stuff today.

115
00:10:08,640 --> 00:10:09,840
There's just so much risk there.

116
00:10:10,560 --> 00:10:17,920
But to research creative, especially with competitors and come up with a hypothesis and things like that, hallucinate all you want.

117
00:10:18,080 --> 00:10:19,200
We're going to go test that anyway.

118
00:10:19,760 --> 00:10:24,640
And the ultimate deciding factor is Meta's algorithm that's going to pick the winning creative anyway.

119
00:10:24,800 --> 00:10:26,400
So you're just like generating ideas.

120
00:10:26,800 --> 00:10:33,600
Hallucination is not going to break things, cost us tens of thousands of dollars if we're trying to buy ads based on it or whatever.

121
00:10:34,080 --> 00:10:35,360
You know who Jason Lemkin is?

122
00:10:35,440 --> 00:10:36,160
He's the Saster.

123
00:10:36,480 --> 00:10:37,120
Oh yeah, yeah.

124
00:10:37,200 --> 00:10:38,480
He's an investor in RevenueCat.

125
00:10:38,560 --> 00:10:38,880
Yeah, very.

126
00:10:39,040 --> 00:10:39,600
He's busy.

127
00:10:39,680 --> 00:10:40,080
Oh wow.

128
00:10:40,480 --> 00:10:45,840
So he was chronicling his experience with Replit, like building an app, vibe coding an app from scratch on Reddit.

129
00:10:45,920 --> 00:10:47,920
And he was just like every day, he was kind of almost like journaling.

130
00:10:48,160 --> 00:10:49,280
Like, here's what I did today.

131
00:10:49,600 --> 00:10:55,120
And then one day he's like, okay projects over replit deleted my production database.

132
00:10:55,600 --> 00:10:58,080
And like, he's like, I'd have to start over.

133
00:10:58,240 --> 00:11:00,160
Like, so I'm, I'm giving up.

134
00:11:00,320 --> 00:11:01,040
That kind of stuff happens.

135
00:11:01,200 --> 00:11:04,640
I mean, I think these tools do really well at very specific discrete tasks.

136
00:11:04,800 --> 00:11:06,480
They don't do well when those tasks are chained together.

137
00:11:06,640 --> 00:11:12,240
And like, there's this idea of like temperature and machine learning, like you want to add a little bit of noise whenever the type of output is being selected.

138
00:11:12,320 --> 00:11:17,440
Like with an LLM, essentially what you're doing is you're doing this, this very like conditional probability to predict the next word.

139
00:11:17,520 --> 00:11:17,680
Right.

140
00:11:17,760 --> 00:11:29,280
And if you think about like attention and like the transformer mechanism, like what the big kind of innovation there is was it could look at like a very long context chain and figure out how each word sort of affected what words come next.

141
00:11:29,520 --> 00:11:33,520
But when you do that, you still add in like a little bit of noise to determine like what the next word could be.

142
00:11:33,680 --> 00:11:36,800
Now, you imagine like you're doing that and then you're stacking that.

143
00:11:36,960 --> 00:11:45,280
And so like when you give it like especially like a very complex task, predicting the next word is not that difficult, but like a very complex task, you stack that noise, that stochasticity, it compounds.

144
00:11:45,520 --> 00:11:48,400
And that's where you get like, hey, here's a 40-step process.

145
00:11:49,120 --> 00:11:53,360
Every step in the way, there's a little bit of stochasticity, a little bit of randomness that's being thrown in there.

146
00:11:53,440 --> 00:11:55,680
And like, by the time you get to the fourth, it's like a game of telephone.

147
00:11:55,760 --> 00:12:00,400
The way I like to approach these tasks is like very discreet and I'm intermediating everything, right?

148
00:12:00,640 --> 00:12:01,280
Here's the output.

149
00:12:01,360 --> 00:12:05,760
Okay, I'm taking that output and then I'm kind of checking it and I'm giving that back to you and I'm saying, now do the next thing.

150
00:12:06,160 --> 00:12:17,760
And so I think we're still in that phase where there needs to be guardrails, either human oversight or just throw it at something where the downside risk is pretty limited and contained.

151
00:12:18,160 --> 00:12:18,240
Yeah.

152
00:12:18,720 --> 00:12:19,840
No, that's fantastic.

153
00:12:20,080 --> 00:12:26,320
Any other specific examples before we move on to the other questions of things you see working today within those limitations.

154
00:12:26,960 --> 00:12:32,480
When you talk to companies, especially in the app world, about the use of AI, they immediately jump to creative.

155
00:12:32,720 --> 00:12:36,480
And I think that's probably the least valuable place to apply this.

156
00:12:36,800 --> 00:12:45,760
I mean, yes, you can go from like ten creatives to two hundred, but a lot of times it's just people taking their ten creatives and those themselves are variants and they're getting two hundred variants of one concept, right?

157
00:12:46,080 --> 00:12:47,520
That's not actually doing anything for you.

158
00:12:47,920 --> 00:12:52,160
There's just decreasing returns on taking twenty variants of one concept and going to two hundred.

159
00:12:52,480 --> 00:12:54,560
And they're like vanishingly small gains.

160
00:12:54,560 --> 00:12:55,120
Small games, right?

161
00:12:55,440 --> 00:12:57,680
What you really care about is the concept.

162
00:12:58,480 --> 00:13:06,880
And actually, it's coming up with concepts that you yourself couldn't come up with, because if you could just come up with them, the AI is not doing that much more to kind of influence the performance.

163
00:13:07,120 --> 00:13:12,640
Sort of like aha moment with this was the last company I worked at, I built this tool called Draper, and that's just what it did.

164
00:13:13,360 --> 00:13:15,120
It just created variants of ads.

165
00:13:15,280 --> 00:13:18,160
And this was like 2018, so people were not really talking about AI at that point.

166
00:13:18,320 --> 00:13:19,520
This wasn't even like machine learning.

167
00:13:19,600 --> 00:13:22,560
It was just created a bunch of variants, like all permutations of these different ads.

168
00:13:22,800 --> 00:13:26,640
What I would do is we would just deploy these on Facebook all the time.

169
00:13:26,800 --> 00:13:28,800
Like there's just this constant cycle of this deployment.

170
00:13:28,960 --> 00:13:32,240
And then we had like a stand up every, every week with the whole company.

171
00:13:32,400 --> 00:13:34,400
And I would say, here's the ad that worked the best this week.

172
00:13:34,640 --> 00:13:35,920
I have no idea what it's going to be.

173
00:13:36,320 --> 00:13:36,480
Right?

174
00:13:36,800 --> 00:13:38,320
But like no person touched that.

175
00:13:38,480 --> 00:13:40,240
That was just this process running in the background.

176
00:13:40,320 --> 00:13:47,600
If you could mass experiment like that, then you could find and then Facebook was at that point, they were still, VO was like a year old.

177
00:13:47,920 --> 00:13:52,560
And so that was the way that they did the audience pairing was VO based on the on the, the sort of value estimate.

178
00:13:53,040 --> 00:13:55,440
I couldn't foresee what audience this was going to be targeted to anyway.

179
00:13:55,520 --> 00:14:01,520
Like, let me just feed the beastast with as much creative as they need and they find those audiences, those high value audiences and they test all this different stuff.

180
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:03,920
That was the big awake, like aha moment for me.

181
00:14:04,320 --> 00:14:06,800
I shouldn't have any kind of preconceived notions about what's going to work.

182
00:14:07,360 --> 00:14:19,840
I actually posted about this maybe a month ago and I got a lot of pushback, but my point was given the use of Pmax, it's pretty text based, but Advantage plus, like there's no point for you to try to interpret why an ad won or didn't win.

183
00:14:20,560 --> 00:14:21,120
There's no point.

184
00:14:21,440 --> 00:14:31,920
What you should be interpreting is if when you get a win or the win rate increases, the process worked took a new input and that's the learning, right?

185
00:14:32,160 --> 00:14:34,560
But it's not the output because that was random.

186
00:14:34,800 --> 00:14:35,760
That was utterly random.

187
00:14:35,920 --> 00:14:37,200
Why that worked was utterly random.

188
00:14:37,440 --> 00:14:41,920
If you try to sort of deconstruct it and take a learning from that, you're just wasting your time.

189
00:14:42,240 --> 00:14:47,600
What you should do is, okay, how am I sort of changing the inputs such that I'm getting a higher win rate and let the machine do its thing?

190
00:14:47,920 --> 00:14:49,040
That output is irrelevant.

191
00:14:49,360 --> 00:14:52,240
That output is cannot be interpreted by you.

192
00:14:52,720 --> 00:14:53,680
You can't understand it.

193
00:14:53,920 --> 00:14:55,280
You can't understand why that worked.

194
00:14:55,440 --> 00:14:55,920
Don't even try.

195
00:14:56,400 --> 00:14:59,120
If it worked though, what did you change about the inpputs?

196
00:14:59,360 --> 00:15:00,720
And that's what you learned, right?

197
00:15:01,040 --> 00:15:02,240
