00:31:33,520 --> 00:31:34,480
But you know what didn't exist?

384
00:31:34,800 --> 00:31:35,200
D to C.

385
00:31:35,440 --> 00:31:37,680
You couldn't have D to C without personalized advertising.

386
00:31:38,320 --> 00:31:40,400
It would just not be possible for the reasons that you pointed out.

387
00:31:40,640 --> 00:31:41,360
It's too niche.

388
00:31:41,680 --> 00:31:42,400
It's too niche.

389
00:31:42,560 --> 00:31:46,080
You can't do a TV ad campaign, a national TV ad campaign for this niche product.

390
00:31:46,400 --> 00:31:47,200
The economics won't work.

391
00:31:47,520 --> 00:31:50,400
But if I could reach the individuals that would find this relevant.

392
00:31:50,480 --> 00:31:54,320
And by the way, when you reach those people, the click the rates are still under five percent, right?

393
00:31:54,640 --> 00:31:56,640
And people look at that as an indictment.

394
00:31:57,040 --> 00:31:58,560
No, that is not an indictment.

395
00:31:58,800 --> 00:32:04,320
That's showing you the kind of natural sort of reluctance or sort of friction there is to advertising in the first place.

396
00:32:04,880 --> 00:32:08,560
If there was actual manipulation happening, those click to rates would be 100%.

397
00:32:09,600 --> 00:32:18,720
If this person is deemed relevant and I was manipulating them to do something they wouldn't otherwise do and they don't want to do, then the click to rate would be 100%, the conversion rate would be 100%, it's sub 5%.

398
00:32:19,360 --> 00:32:21,280
On a good day, it's 4%.

399
00:32:21,600 --> 00:32:26,480
Like for most products, it could be sub 2, sub 1, and those could be profitable campaigns.

400
00:32:27,120 --> 00:32:31,120
But the thing is, like this enabled new sectors of the economy, and that is great.

401
00:32:31,120 --> 00:32:36,640
And there are bad aspects, of course, but you don't throw the baby out with the bathwater.

402
00:32:36,800 --> 00:32:44,080
You identify the things that you want to remedy and you, in a surgical way, you sort of remove those from the workflow.

403
00:32:44,240 --> 00:32:46,160
You don't just throw everything out and trash everything out.

404
00:32:46,320 --> 00:32:52,000
And like people push back when I make these kind of sweeping statements and they say, that's a straw man.

405
00:32:52,240 --> 00:32:54,160
Nobody's actually looking to kill personalized.

406
00:32:54,560 --> 00:32:55,040
Yes, they are.

407
00:32:55,680 --> 00:32:56,160
Yes, they are.

408
00:32:57,040 --> 00:33:02,320
There is a bill that was just resuscitated called the banning personalized advertising.

409
00:33:02,800 --> 00:33:03,040
Act.

410
00:33:03,440 --> 00:33:04,560
They did ban it in the EU.

411
00:33:05,040 --> 00:33:05,200
They did.

412
00:33:05,440 --> 00:33:08,800
Effectively, it's banned for the biggest platforms, the gatekeepers.

413
00:33:09,120 --> 00:33:13,040
And so yes, there are people that want to ban personalized advertising.

414
00:33:13,360 --> 00:33:22,480
I'm the one who started the conversation to D to C, but to be honest, it's the app industry, the proliferation of the app industry and subscription apps specifically.

415
00:33:23,200 --> 00:33:38,560
Meta and Google deserve almost as much, if not more, credit for the proliferation of apps and the variety of apps that we see today and so many profitable app businesses for the same reason that they empowered D2C.

416
00:33:38,720 --> 00:33:44,880
It's like they allowed app developers to reach those audiences more effectively, more efficiently.

417
00:33:45,600 --> 00:33:55,040
What we see today as the app industry, I think owes a debt of gratitude to Meta and to Google because they empowered it just like they empowered D2C.

418
00:33:55,600 --> 00:34:02,880
Well, you and I could talk about these sorts of things for hours, but I did want to get to the questions since we did allow people to submit questions.

419
00:34:03,040 --> 00:34:03,600
Question one, Scott.

420
00:34:03,760 --> 00:34:04,080
Question one.

421
00:34:04,080 --> 00:34:04,080
Question one.

422
00:34:04,080 --> 00:34:05,920
Question one and how we're in.

423
00:34:07,280 --> 00:34:18,000
Given the dominance of a few paid UA channels, how risky is it for a fintech subscription app, and I would say any subscription app, to have over eighty percent of its spend on Meta and Google?

424
00:34:18,480 --> 00:34:22,000
What practical diversification levers actually work in this vertical?

425
00:34:22,320 --> 00:34:23,280
So first, is it risky?

426
00:34:23,440 --> 00:34:25,360
And then, if so, how would you diversify?

427
00:34:25,840 --> 00:34:29,600
So I wrote a piece about this a while back, a couple of years ago.

428
00:34:29,760 --> 00:34:35,040
And like the point I make was, I think people feel compelled to diversify because they're not.

429
00:34:35,040 --> 00:34:40,000
Because they have this kind of abstract notion that being totally concentrated in one or two channels is a bad thing.

430
00:34:40,480 --> 00:34:46,160
And yeah, there's risk there, but the reality is that diversification adds a lot of overhead.

431
00:34:46,640 --> 00:34:52,320
It adds overhead in terms of having to do data integrations and having to create new creative formats, right?

432
00:34:52,480 --> 00:34:58,240
And having to, you know, a lot of times these companies, each platform has a kind of a different way that the ads are exposed.

433
00:34:58,720 --> 00:35:01,040
And so you have to kind of accommodate your measurement to that.

434
00:35:01,600 --> 00:35:05,120
And so diversifying for the sake of diversification is often a bad idea.

435
00:35:05,680 --> 00:35:08,240
And the question is like, and then how much could I spend?

436
00:35:08,400 --> 00:35:08,560
Right?

437
00:35:08,720 --> 00:35:15,520
So like I might on board, like, a lot of times you hear this kind of like common refrain of like, well, the performance is great but at really low spend.

438
00:35:15,760 --> 00:35:17,280
Well, okay, but that's actually not great.

439
00:35:17,520 --> 00:35:21,520
Like what I really care about is my spend adjusted ROAS, right?

440
00:35:21,760 --> 00:35:25,360
And not just that the ROAS is high on this particular channel at low levels of spend.

441
00:35:25,760 --> 00:35:36,320
Because could I have taken all of that overhead, eliminated it for supporting that new channel, and then allocated that budget to Meta or Google and seen the same level of ROAS?

442
00:35:36,720 --> 00:35:38,800
Because if I did, I'm actually worse off if I could have.

443
00:35:39,280 --> 00:35:44,880
So it's actually when you have a new channel, the ROAS doesn't have to meet the ROAS of the other channels that could have absorbed that budget.

444
00:35:45,040 --> 00:35:48,320
It has to exceed that because you're supporting a new channel.

445
00:35:48,800 --> 00:35:52,400
And so I think like diversifying for the sake of diversifying is often a mistake.

446
00:35:52,720 --> 00:35:57,600
You diversify when you've reached saturation on the existing channels, I think.

447
00:35:57,840 --> 00:35:59,040
And then you look for other channels.

448
00:35:59,360 --> 00:36:07,200
Or when you feel like there's some kind of interaction effect that that channel could produce that boosts the performance on other channels.

449
00:36:07,680 --> 00:36:08,960
And that oftentimes is the case, right?

450
00:36:09,200 --> 00:36:13,360
Oftentimes that's the benefit of running on some other channels, particularly if it's more like brand oriented channel.

451
00:36:13,520 --> 00:36:15,520
So I think that's just the way I like to think about it.

452
00:36:15,840 --> 00:36:19,120
I wrote a piece a couple of weeks ago called Optimization Models in Digital Advertising.

453
00:36:19,200 --> 00:36:23,520
I talk about optimizing towards ROAS and optimizing towards ROAS adjusted spend.

454
00:36:23,520 --> 00:36:24,560
And they're very different things, right?

455
00:36:24,720 --> 00:36:33,360
And so if I'm optimizing towards ROAS, then I really want to keep spend as low as possible spread across many different channels because I'll get the maximum ROAS per channel, right?

456
00:36:33,440 --> 00:36:36,000
Because the ROAS and the spend tend to move in opposite directions, right?

457
00:36:36,160 --> 00:36:41,600
But that's oftentimes, that's not really what I'm optimizing towards maximizing spend with a ROAS constraint.

458
00:36:41,760 --> 00:36:44,400
And in that case, what you want to do is this what I call the waterfall method.

459
00:36:44,480 --> 00:36:52,640
So max out the biggest channel, right, until it hits my ROAS threshold, then move on to channel two, which would be smaller, like, potential spend there.

460
00:36:53,600 --> 00:36:57,280
Max out channel two until it hits my ROAS threshold, move on to channel three.

461
00:36:57,680 --> 00:37:02,960
That's always the approach that I recommend companies to take because it minimizes overhead and complexity.

462
00:37:03,760 --> 00:37:08,240
And so what do you see as the risk or do you think it's just actually not risky?

463
00:37:08,640 --> 00:37:10,320
The risk is just missed opportunity.ity.

464
00:37:11,360 --> 00:37:18,160
How would you classify risk and how people should think about the risk of being so dependent on one or two channels?

465
00:37:18,960 --> 00:37:20,400
So, yeah, it's missed opportunity.

466
00:37:21,040 --> 00:37:33,920
You could call it opportunity cost risk, but what most people kind of mean when they talk about the risk there is just that performance degrades, and if I've got five channels and one of them bombs, then okay, like there's twenty percent of my spend at risk.

467
00:37:34,080 --> 00:37:40,160
But if I've got one channel and it absolutely tanks, which can happen, then that's all my spend.

468
00:37:40,480 --> 00:37:43,840
Now the issue here is these things tend to be pretty correlated.

469
00:37:44,080 --> 00:37:46,320
Like why would performance bomb on a channel?

470
00:37:46,640 --> 00:37:49,440
Probably because a competitor came in and is outbidding you everywhere.

471
00:37:49,680 --> 00:37:54,080
And if they're outbidding you on Facebook, they're probably outbidding you on Google and Snap and TikTok and wherever else.

472
00:37:54,320 --> 00:37:56,880
So the issue is I think the risk is not necessarily per channel.

473
00:37:57,040 --> 00:38:02,640
That's what I think a lot of times people think about it that way because everyone has had experiences where like, hey man, what happened to Facebook yesterday?

474
00:38:02,880 --> 00:38:08,400
Like, you know, click the rates were cut in half or they could have cut down by two thirds or whatever for whatever reason and there was just like a blip.

475
00:38:08,640 --> 00:38:21,040
But if you're thinking like a structural change, a structural change of performance that probably would be correlated across every channel because there's either just a change in consumer sentiment or more commonly a competitor came in and just crowded you out.

476
00:38:21,440 --> 00:38:30,400
Okay, what's the single biggest pitfall you see across mobile growth teams right now, something that even sophisticated companies consistently overlook?

477
00:38:30,880 --> 00:38:41,280
The thing that I see most commonly is just a very kind of chaotic approach to measurement, just lacking any set of sort of coherence that could materialize in a couple of different ways.

478
00:38:41,440 --> 00:38:49,040
Like, either I see people using competing tools essentially not really knowing which one to trust or how to interpret the output of one relative to the other.

479
00:38:49,680 --> 00:38:53,440
I see this as just misalignment across the various stakeholders, right?

480
00:38:53,680 --> 00:39:02,480
So like often times finance and UA or UA and product and not really being totally aligned on like what good looks like, what these metrics should look like for success.

481
00:39:03,280 --> 00:39:08,320
I see it as having a bunch of tools that aren't working in sort of concert, right?

482
00:39:08,480 --> 00:39:10,480
Like they're just a couple of different data points, right?

483
00:39:10,640 --> 00:39:12,720
Like I don't know how to interpret them as an ensemble.

484
00:39:12,880 --> 00:39:14,320
I'm just looking at the individual ones.

485
00:39:14,400 --> 00:39:17,600
I don't really know what these things mean as a whole.

486
00:39:18,080 --> 00:39:20,960
I call it measurement disorganization is the most common thing I see.

487
00:39:21,920 --> 00:39:26,960
I think the way to overcome that is like this is not a satisfying answer and it sucks as a process.

488
00:39:27,600 --> 00:39:35,760
I don't do that much consulting anymore, but this was probably the most common project I was asked to come in and it's just it's really, really challenging and it's stressful.

489
00:39:36,240 --> 00:39:44,240
Getting all the stakeholders together and then just coming up with some sort of plan that satisfies everyone, some sort of model for measurement.

490
00:39:44,720 --> 00:39:48,880
When I say model, I don't mean like a machine learning model or whatever, a regression model.

491
00:39:49,120 --> 00:39:57,440
I mean like an operational model for how we're all aligned around what success looks like and like all our needs are being met by this measurement apparatus.

492
00:39:57,600 --> 00:40:10,160
Because I think one thing that companies just tend to underestimate is the fact that your measurement model, your measurement system is essentially like the heartbeat of the company.

493
00:40:11,120 --> 00:40:12,160
Everything flows from that.

494
00:40:12,560 --> 00:40:21,680
You really need to be doing it correctly and in a way that is's credible, but also in a way that sort of like everyone understands and appreciates, right?

495
00:40:22,000 --> 00:40:24,080
And that serves their different use cases.

496
00:40:24,720 --> 00:40:29,360
It's like getting a group of people together in a room and just finding alignment on that.

497
00:40:30,320 --> 00:40:31,040
That's the solution.

498
00:40:31,680 --> 00:40:32,400
And that sucks.

499
00:40:32,720 --> 00:40:35,360
That's difficult, people-oriented work.

500
00:40:35,840 --> 00:40:40,320
It's not like, oh, well, we'll come up with a new machine learning model or we'll build this new dashboard.

501
00:40:40,720 --> 00:40:43,840
It's like, let's get a bunch of people together, understand their needs.

502
00:40:44,480 --> 00:40:48,720
The CFO's got totally different needs than the UA teams team and the UA team has totally different needs than the product team.

503
00:40:48,960 --> 00:40:55,760
And let's just get everyone in a room and understand what they need to receive as output and also get everyone to agree on what good looks like.

504
00:40:56,160 --> 00:40:56,880
That's a great answer.

505
00:40:57,040 --> 00:41:12,240
I I wouldn't have guessed you would go into the people side of things, but I mean, we're just bags of meat making decisions and that's that's a huge point of friction, a huge point of challenge and disagreement.

506
00:41:12,400 --> 00:41:17,520
So, you know, one of the things we actually see at RevenueCat is we sometimes have a marketing team.

507
00:41:18,160 --> 00:41:24,800
comes to us specifically because the engineering team just won't align with them on their priorities.

508
00:41:25,040 --> 00:41:32,000
Like the engineering and product teams have their own priorities and UA and the marketing teams are just like, Hey, go figure it out.

509
00:41:32,160 --> 00:41:33,280
They're left out on an island.

510
00:41:33,440 --> 00:41:34,880
They can't get engineering resources.

511
00:41:35,120 --> 00:41:39,280
They can't get time with the product teams to push to get the engineering resources.

512
00:41:39,600 --> 00:41:48,160
And so, yeah, it's like, it's a problem we see in prospects coming into RevenueCat trying to solve the engineering problems because their engineering teams just won't give them the time of the day.

513
00:41:48,880 --> 00:42:06,000
The canonical example in my mind, and like I've experienced this, and I have like Vietnam style flashbacks of this very difficult, challenging, human-oriented problem is product team on board a different LTV model or a different LTV product because they don't trust the one the UA team is using.

514
00:42:06,400 --> 00:42:08,160
That's the kiss of death for productivity.

515
00:42:08,320 --> 00:42:11,520
You're going to spend the next two months arguing and that relationship's over.

516
00:42:12,560 --> 00:42:13,120
Yeah, man.

517
00:42:13,440 --> 00:42:14,880
And it's a great place to go.

518
00:42:15,040 --> 00:42:16,240
Like solve your people's problems.

519
00:42:16,560 --> 00:42:18,000
Technology's not going to fix everything.

520
00:42:18,240 --> 00:42:19,680
You need to solve the people's problems in the UA.

521
00:42:19,680 --> 00:42:24,000
the people problems in concert with solving the tech problems and data problems and all the other problems.

522
00:42:24,560 --> 00:42:25,440
Okay, next question.

523
00:42:25,760 --> 00:42:42,080
In subscription apps, and then again, this maybe was the same person, especially in fintech, but this probably applies to all apps, you often have to make budget allocation or bid changes before key metrics like LTV retention or incrementality are fully baked, sometimes within days of launch.

524
00:42:42,400 --> 00:42:47,040
How do you balance speed versus accuracy in those early optimization decisions?

525
00:42:47,440 --> 00:42:50,400
And what frameworks do you recommend for making confident calls?

526
00:42:50,960 --> 00:42:51,760
with incomplete data?

527
00:42:52,320 --> 00:42:53,200
That's a really good question.

528
00:42:53,840 --> 00:42:54,720
Yeah, that's a good question.

529
00:42:54,960 --> 00:42:56,000
So a couple of things, right?

530
00:42:56,240 --> 00:43:04,320
So one is I wrote this piece some time ago called It's time to retire the LTV metric and it's a little bit of a clickbait headline.

531
00:43:05,040 --> 00:43:05,920
I don't really mean that.

532
00:43:06,480 --> 00:43:15,280
What I mean is what you often see teams doing is like they'll try to calculate a terminal LTV on the basis of like ten days of data or something.

533
00:43:15,680 --> 00:43:16,960
You only launch once, right?

534
00:43:17,200 --> 00:43:18,080
But like a product.

535
00:43:18,640 --> 00:43:21,040
And so you can just kind of wait and extend the soft launch.

536
00:43:21,440 --> 00:43:25,920
But like we were talking about like a campaign level LTV, it's like, well, okay, I'm not going to get that quickly.

537
00:43:26,400 --> 00:43:34,160
And so what I like to do is just try to because again, ad spend and ROAS tend to be inversely correlated.

538
00:43:34,240 --> 00:43:36,640
So as ad spend increases, ROAS goes down, right?

539
00:43:36,800 --> 00:43:39,440
And so what I would like to do is just kind of prove out these frontiers.

540
00:43:40,080 --> 00:43:44,160
And so I'm going to spend five k a day or whatever.

541
00:43:44,240 --> 00:43:46,720
And, you know, if I'm hitting 150 ROAS, great.

542
00:43:47,440 --> 00:44:03,440
And then what I'll do is let those cohorts age, understand how they progress, understand what their day 20 ROAS is, understand what the day 30 ROAS is, and like just build more and more cohorts where I can track them over time, and then I'll push that ROAS frontier out.

543
00:44:03,520 --> 00:44:08,240
So like now I'm not trying to hit 150 on day three or 200 on day three.

544
00:44:08,400 --> 00:44:13,840
I'll like, let me just see and actually these cohorts seem to be progressing to where they're at like, you know, 150 by day 30 or something.

545
00:44:14,080 --> 00:44:14,640
Okay, that's great.

546
00:44:14,720 --> 00:44:17,600
Now what I'm going to do is I'm going to push and then I change my bid accordingly, right?

547
00:44:17,920 --> 00:44:18,720
And then grow the budget.

548
00:44:19,040 --> 00:44:21,840
And then what I'm going to do is track the cohorts more and see where they land at day 60.

549
00:44:22,000 --> 00:44:23,680
It's like, okay, well, that's  120.

550
00:44:23,760 --> 00:44:26,560
So I'm going to increase the budget more and then I'll decrease the ROAS.

551
00:44:26,640 --> 00:44:29,760
And so then what I really care about is like where does that land at like 110 or something.

552
00:44:29,920 --> 00:44:42,960
But really what I'm trying to do is just iteratively progress that frontier of my ROAS target starting from a place where like, yeah, okay, if I can't hit 150 day ROAS at day seven with very low spend, probably won't be able to grow this.

553
00:44:43,520 --> 00:44:46,880
And so it's back to the drawing board with whatever I'm adjusting.

554
00:44:47,200 --> 00:44:50,960
That's the way I think is the right way to approach this, especially like in a launch phase.

555
00:44:51,040 --> 00:44:55,520
If you're just talking about like launch, like fighting new campaigns, really what you're tal talking about is the performance of the creative.

556
00:44:56,000 --> 00:45:00,560
And it's a question of, well, when to kill a creative as soon as it's obviously not a winner.

557
00:45:00,720 --> 00:45:02,080
And that's oftentimes very quickly.

558
00:45:02,240 --> 00:45:03,600
Like the same day, it could be next day.

559
00:45:03,840 --> 00:45:05,920
This is getting no delivery, not a winner.

560
00:45:06,400 --> 00:45:10,160
Maybe you could perform at the average or whatever, but it's not going to grow my ad spend.

561
00:45:10,320 --> 00:45:11,680
So it's a loser and I kill it.

562
00:45:12,000 --> 00:45:15,920
The way I approach creative testing is really just I'm trying to identify losers as quickly as possible.

563
00:45:16,160 --> 00:45:18,800
The winners take time to prove out, but the losers are pretty quick to prove out.

564
00:45:19,200 --> 00:45:20,080
Yeah, great answer.

565
00:45:20,640 --> 00:45:27,120
Next up, what's one opportunity for growth you think most companiesies in the app industry are missing right now?

566
00:45:27,360 --> 00:45:32,160
Either because it's too early, too messy, or doesn't fit into standard user acquisition playbooks.

567
00:45:32,640 --> 00:45:37,520
The real answer is the opportunity for growth is just that your measurement doesn't support true growth.

568
00:45:38,160 --> 00:45:42,320
It's broken or it's flimsy, and so you're just really trying to replicate what you've done in the past.

569
00:45:43,120 --> 00:45:52,640
If you don't believe that your measurement can adapt to new channels, new sources, new ad types, you're just going to do what you've been doing, and by definition you won't grow.

570
00:45:53,920 --> 00:45:58,960
If you need to break out of that, you need to build truly robust incremental focused measurement.

571
00:45:59,360 --> 00:46:00,240
And that's the challenge, right?

572
00:46:00,480 --> 00:46:03,760
The challenge for the human reason we talked about is a challenge, it's a technical challenge too.

573
00:46:04,080 --> 00:46:05,440
So that's the real answer.

574
00:46:05,840 --> 00:46:11,200
And then assuming that's in place, then you have the world as your billboard, right?

575
00:46:11,440 --> 00:46:23,120
Like I can advertise in influencer channels, I can do out of home, I could do digital out of home, I could do CTV, I could do podcasts, I could do all these things that are supported by the measurement.

576
00:46:23,360 --> 00:46:37,680
But oftentimes that lack of growth, that stasis is just a function of like of like, I don't trust the measurement to capably run attribution on these new channels or interpret the changes across the portfolio in a reliable way.

577
00:46:37,840 --> 00:46:39,760
And for that reason, I'm just going to keep everything the same.

578
00:46:40,400 --> 00:46:40,560
Right?

579
00:46:40,720 --> 00:46:42,320
And so that's, that's oftentimes what I see.

580
00:46:42,480 --> 00:46:47,040
It's like people are just paralyzed by as a function of their measurement not being robust.

581
00:46:47,760 --> 00:46:52,400
The fundamental problem in marketing, uh, half my marketing is performing.

582
